00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.540
So let's dive in.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.750
So let's go through the
history of storage and database

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:42.595
products.

00:01:42.595 --> 00:01:46.700
So if I go kind of
all the way back

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.270
from a storage point
of view, originally,

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:53.260
when you attached
storage to machines,

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:54.680
it was physically attached.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:55.910
So this is direct.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:57.740
It's called direct
attached storage.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.150
You take a hard disk.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.970
You physically attach
it to a computer.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:06.440
And that's the storage that that
computer has available to it.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.759
The application, the
file system, the storage,

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:13.950
they're all on a single
machine, a single device

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.840
that your application runs on.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.250
So, obviously, this has
a lot of limitations,

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.057
but it's kind of
where things started.

00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:22.640
And these obviously
still exist today.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.710
So direct attached
storage physically

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.899
attach to the device.

00:02:26.899 --> 00:02:28.940
Obviously, you start to
run into some limitations

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.520
with this, like, huh, what
happens if I need more storage?

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.080
What happens if I need to share
my storage between servers?

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.790
What do I do then?

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.310
This is where you now need
to start to think about,

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:42.500
where is my compute?

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.090
Where is my storage?

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.260
And what role is
the network playing

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.340
in connecting these together?

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:54.200
And two different
models emerged here.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.410
One is a Storage Area
Network, or a SAN.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:03.590
What this lets you do is it lets
you move the underlying block

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:06.350
storage off of the
machine and put it

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:11.420
into a shared piece of
hardware that often offers

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.190
really great
performance, tiering

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:15.060
between different
classes of storage,

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.500
whether it's SSD
devices or hard disks.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.530
But the file system
and the application

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:27.590
is still living on the
server or the client.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:29.870
So you can't really
share that storage

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:33.290
between different applications
or between different computers,

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.640
because the file system
it's operating in

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.910
is still out there
in that server.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:44.030
Another alternative is the
Network Attached Storage.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.910
And in this model, one of
the most common examples

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.250
is something like a fileshare.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:54.110
So here, the server
is a fileshare server.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:55.700
You mount it over the networks.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.235
You go into your
operating system.

00:03:58.235 --> 00:04:03.380
You mount a directory or you
mount a share over the network.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:07.070
And the file system is
remote from your computer

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:07.910
over the network.

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:09.770
And what that lets
you do is now start

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:12.750
to share files between
many different computers.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:17.990
So if two clients want
to operate over a shared

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.480
directory and a
fileshare, they can do it

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:26.780
by using a NAS
solution to do that.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.520
And then these are probably
the most two common.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.270
And actually still today, in
an enterprise on-premises IT

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.970
infrastructure, these are the
most common forms of storage

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.190
that you'll see.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:41.000
And more recently, object
storage really started

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.530
to emerge as a third
form of storage

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.330
for when you have
really large amounts

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:51.560
of unstructured storage,
media files or other assets

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.290
that don't need the
super-high performance

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:58.715
and often costly solutions
that are often provided by SANs

00:04:58.715 --> 00:05:04.910
and NAS, but gives you sort of
low-cost, large-object storage.

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.800
And these solutions,
all of these solutions

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.860
are available in enterprises.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:11.640
And then as you
look at the cloud,

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.460
the products that
you'll map to and you'll

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:18.740
hear me talking about
today for block storage

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:22.010
are persistent disk
in both standard

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:24.050
and SSD versions and local SSDs.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:27.520
So this is our block storage
product for attaching to VM.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.940
For Network Attached Storage,
so for a file system,

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:34.940
we announced recently--

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:36.400
as recently as last month--

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.710
Cloud Filestore, which is now
in beta, which is a file storage

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:44.499
product for NAS solutions.

00:05:44.499 --> 00:05:46.540
We also have great
partnerships with NetApp, EMC,

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:49.550
and Elastifile, who are also
offering their solutions

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:51.200
as part of GCP.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.170
And then for object
storage, you'll

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:58.960
hear me talk about cloud storage
as our object storage solution.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.900
Maybe one thing to note,
actually, in the cloud,

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.930
object storage actually sort
of took off disproportionately

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:10.150
to the on premises use case.

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:12.929
As cloud providers started to
bring storage to the cloud,

00:06:12.929 --> 00:06:15.470
actually, many of them started
with object storage solutions.

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:17.570
And so the object
storage use cases

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:21.490
have sort of really
ballooned with the cloud.

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:23.890
So this is the sort of evolution
of the different various

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:24.680
storage products.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.142
It's all really to do with,
where's my file system?

00:06:27.142 --> 00:06:27.850
Where's the data?

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:29.050
And where's the network, right?

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:30.675
We'll come back to
that, the importance

00:06:30.675 --> 00:06:34.920
of the network in this a couple
of times through the talk.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:36.790
Databases-- this one's
a little trickier.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:46.150
So the world of databases was
super easy for 20 or 30 years.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:48.730
The idea of a relational
database model

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:50.950
where you have
tables, relationships

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:54.340
between those databases, you'd
run your transactional system,

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:58.660
your banking system
against that database.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:03.190
And if you ever needed to do
reporting or analytics on it,

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:07.480
typically, these systems were a
scale-up, actually often quite

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:11.800
expensive, didn't necessarily
store a huge amount of data

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.030
because of the cost in scale.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.030
And if you wanted to run
reporting and analytics

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:18.340
queries against them,
you really didn't

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.940
want to run that reporting
query against the system that's

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:24.510
doing your transactional
records or system

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:25.510
of record for your bank.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.160
So what you would
typically do is

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.680
you would go through a
process called ETL, so

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.850
Extract, Transform, Load.

00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:34.450
You would lift up
a copy of the data,

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:37.450
and you'd put it in something
called a data warehouse.

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:40.960
And the data warehouse
was a system specifically

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:46.180
designed for high-performance
queries that aggregated

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:47.320
large amounts of data.

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:50.055
So in the process of moving
the data from your orders

00:07:50.055 --> 00:07:52.180
and records and customers
table, you'd move it over

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:53.570
to a data warehouse.

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:55.630
You'd transform the
data in a way that

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:57.610
met the needs of
the queries that you

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:00.020
were going to run against it.

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:02.410
And that's where you would
run your reports and analytics

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:03.640
from.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:06.610
And those various OLAP
and other technologies

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.500
that came along to
help accelerate that

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:09.900
and make that super efficient.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:13.390
And for 20, 30 years,
major on-prem--

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:17.530
Oracle, Microsoft, IBM
built huge, huge businesses

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:20.530
out of this ecosystem.

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:25.790
And for 30 years,
that was good enough.

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:30.640
And about 10, 15 years ago,
a whole bunch of things

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:36.700
started to happen that caused
a lot of chaos, confusion,

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:41.500
made choices for you
all harder, right?

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:42.380
So what happened?

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:47.160
Well, as the world
became more connected,

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.650
the internet
emerged, IoT emerged.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.990
The amount of data
that's getting generated

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:56.760
and that there's an opportunity
to derive value from just

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:57.670
exploded.

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:00.567
I mean, if it's media
assets and other things,

00:09:00.567 --> 00:09:03.150
you might put them into object
storage systems and serve them.

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:06.550
But if you want to start to
do analytics over log files,

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:09.780
and other really
large semi-structured

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.810
or unstructured
data, there really

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:14.610
wasn't an analytics
alternative for you.

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:16.830
If you put it in your
warehouse, it wasn't really

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:18.720
super well designed for that.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:19.920
And it certainly didn't--

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.440
from a cost point of view, it
would get pretty prohibitively

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.180
expensive pretty quickly.

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:30.210
So actually, Google,
some time ago,

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:33.750
introduced this paradigm called
MapReduce, which basically

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:36.450
said, drop all of your
unstructured data into just

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.000
the cheapest form
of storage possible.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.470
Store large amounts of it.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:45.510
And then run jobs against that
to sort of refine that data

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.520
and structure it,
count words in it

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:51.342
or look through logs for
quick patterns and analytics.

00:09:51.342 --> 00:09:53.800
And it introduced a framework
called MapReduce around that.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:56.730
That was popularized into the
Hadoop Open Source Project.

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:59.310
Companies like
Hortonworks, MapR, Cloudera

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:02.220
took that further and made
commercial distributions

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:03.954
around that.

00:10:03.954 --> 00:10:06.120
So for a while, there was
this interesting dichotomy

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:08.130
in the analytics industry.

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:11.577
The big data vendors will say,
throw away your data warehouse.

00:10:11.577 --> 00:10:12.660
You don't need it anymore.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.240
Do everything in the
big data systems.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.850
And the data warehouse folks
were all very confused, going,

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:19.929
what?

00:10:19.929 --> 00:10:20.970
Why are they saying that?

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:22.178
You don't need those systems.

00:10:22.178 --> 00:10:23.790
Just put it all
in your warehouse.

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:26.440
So that sort of played out
over a couple of years.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.267
Fortunately, now everybody
seems to have settled down,

00:10:29.267 --> 00:10:30.850
and everyone recognizes
that these are

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:32.550
sort of complementary systems.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:35.010
So data lakes are great for
gathering and aggregating

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:36.179
large amounts of data.

00:10:36.179 --> 00:10:38.220
And when you want to serve
it for very particular

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.130
analytics use cases and it
all becomes more structured,

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:43.440
then a data warehouse
fits in well.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.920
So that's the analytics
part of the world.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.910
In terms of storing
records of data,

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:53.070
though, this whole other
paradigm emerged called NoSQL.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.510
And again, a couple
of sort of patterns

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:59.430
that have driven that were
use cases where, hey, I've

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:03.390
got devices sending me
back huge amounts of data.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:05.305
I don't really know
the columns that I'm

00:11:05.305 --> 00:11:06.930
going to have for
that data in advance.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:10.592
They might change, or the data
is going to be very sparse.

00:11:10.592 --> 00:11:12.300
You could store some
of that and actually

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:15.870
go quite a long way with a
traditional relational database

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:17.070
management system.

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:20.280
But it sort of became clunky
and a bit sort of overpowered

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:24.180
in some areas and
underpowered in other places.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:27.200
So for something like
large key value stores,

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:33.780
that was a whole other class
of NoSQL products that emerged.

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:35.920
Another pattern
is JSON documents.

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.730
So if you have customer catalogs
or other things where there's

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:42.060
a sort of a hierarchical
structure to the data,

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:45.200
as opposed to a tabular
structure to the data,

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.180
it's just a hard,
round peg, square hole,

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:50.140
to model that into a database.

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:56.500
So a specific class of NoSQL
databases emerged out of that.

00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:00.336
And then the other thing
with regular databases,

00:12:00.336 --> 00:12:01.460
it's kind of hard to scale.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:02.550
They scale up pretty well.

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:05.170
Scaling them out is really hard.

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:08.220
So there's now, at the
sort of bleeding edge--

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:12.210
and Google has some
of these products--

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:14.730
to really help customers
now scale their databases

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:16.470
beyond the traditional
confines of what

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:17.670
they've been able to do.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:19.007
So these have been--

00:12:19.007 --> 00:12:21.090
I'll talk to some of the
specifics of the products

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:23.810
we have on this all in a second.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:25.560
But I just wanted to
take you a little bit

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:29.100
on the journey of
where these came from.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:32.135
And they're actually all still
evolving as they go forward.

00:12:32.135 --> 00:12:34.260
So as I look across all
the different products that

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:37.030
are out there, you
can see some of these

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:38.340
starting to blend and merge.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:40.020
So be prepared for a
little bit of that.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:45.090
But there's a very powerful
set of products now

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:47.200
that are much more
niche to use case.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:51.270
It used to be RDBMS, hammer
hit, use that in all cases.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.820
There's actually more
choice now in terms

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:57.180
of what you can do with the
products and the benefit

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:58.120
you can get from them.

00:12:58.120 --> 00:12:59.910
So let me start
to go through some

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:03.790
of the products in a
little bit more detail.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:07.860
So this is the overall
portfolio that we have for GCP.

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:09.140
So all the way--

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:11.280
I'll start on the right--
so object file and block

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:15.450
products, cloud storage,
persistent disk file store.

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:17.910
Our data warehouse
product is BigQuery.

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:20.490
From the database side,
relational and NoSQL, cloud SQL

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.860
Spanner, data store
Bigtable, and then

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:24.810
for caching in memory,
Cloud Memorystore.

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:27.960
I'll go through each of
these in a bit more detail.

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.510
Before I do that,
though, one of the things

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:36.510
that is another sort of
trend that has emerged

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:39.780
is this ability to separate
storage and compute.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:42.780
One of the reasons storage and
compute need to be often tied

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:46.320
together is obviously just
for performance, right?

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.990
If your storage and
compute is co-located,

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.586
you get much better performance.

00:13:50.586 --> 00:13:51.960
That often means
then it's needed

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:55.260
to be physically co-located,
often onto the same machine.

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:59.680
One of the big things that I
love about Google's product

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:04.230
lineup is we've invested
so deeply in the network

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.780
capability, both
wide area network,

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:10.530
like you might have heard Brad
talk about in this morning's

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:14.610
keynote, but also in data
center network, the network

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:17.280
within a data center.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.700
Our products-- we can actually
separate storage and compute

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:23.520
from each other physically,
and actually not have it

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:26.470
really affect our performance
in a meaningful way.

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:30.060
And one of the things that
this really fundamentally lets

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:34.570
you achieve is you can scale
storage separate from compute,

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:35.080
right?

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:38.160
And this is a fundamentally
important capability

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:40.950
that lets you really think
about and design systems

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:43.500
in a different way, particularly
in the data and analytics

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:44.000
world.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.650
If you can just gather and
store gigantic amounts of data

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:49.804
and storage without having to
size compute as you go along

00:14:49.804 --> 00:14:51.720
with it, even if you
don't know how much of it

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:54.860
you're going to use, it's a
very, very powerful paradigm.

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:56.365
And across all our
products, that's

00:14:56.365 --> 00:14:57.990
one thing that we've
done consistently,

00:14:57.990 --> 00:14:59.990
is separate storage
from compute.

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:02.400
It actually means that the
products that we can offer

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.760
tend to then be fundamentally
simpler because we

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:11.071
made that, effectively, a very
seamless experience for you.

00:15:11.071 --> 00:15:13.320
Let me just dive in a little
bit more on the products.

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:14.730
OK, so cloud storage.

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:20.010
So this is basically our fully
managed object storage system.

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:24.120
Very classic use cases here
is I want to store and serve

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:27.330
media, so videos, images.

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:29.430
I want to store
them, serve them.

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:33.390
I might want to back up our
archive, large amounts of data,

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:35.580
or I might want to
create a data lake

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:38.619
and store large amounts of
data for data processing,

00:15:38.619 --> 00:15:40.410
and bring a selection
of analytics products

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:42.860
to bear on top of that.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:44.820
Used across a whole bunch
of verticals-- media,

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:46.500
mobile, financial
services, health

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:52.560
care-- we see this product used
very broadly across the board.

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:55.380
Some of the key features
about this product.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:57.800
So we offer four
different storage classes.

00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:00.630
So you can choose two
location types, multi-regional

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:02.310
or regional storage.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:04.500
And from there, you
can then tier the data

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:07.940
down into what we call
nearline and coldline.

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:10.380
So this gives you the ability
to have data that's hot,

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:13.230
available to serve
immediately, but tier it down

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:16.500
to colder, cheaper
classes of storage

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:19.950
if you don't need to
access it very often.

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:22.200
And one thing that's very
unique about this product

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.767
is we give you a
single API across all

00:16:25.767 --> 00:16:27.100
those different storage classes.

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:30.660
So once you put an object
into Google Cloud's Storage

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:33.156
and you get an object
ID, behind the scenes,

00:16:33.156 --> 00:16:34.530
you can change
the storage class.

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:35.905
And you can put
it in our coldest

00:16:35.905 --> 00:16:37.920
class of storage coldline.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:39.210
The object ID doesn't change.

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:42.630
And more importantly, if you
ever need to access that data,

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:44.490
your performance in
terms of accessing it

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:45.570
doesn't change, either.

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:48.120
So you basically
get instant access

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:52.560
to your cold archive data, which
is very unique in the industry,

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:55.852
and a super powerful
model for when

00:16:55.852 --> 00:16:57.060
you're building applications.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.610
Because now you can just build
your application once, and not

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:03.240
worry about the storage the
class that it's stored in,

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:05.970
and manage the cost and access
to that storage separate

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:07.650
from the application.

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:10.410
So single API across
all storage classes,

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:13.609
millisecond access to
the first byte of data,

00:17:13.609 --> 00:17:15.599
takes advantage of
our wide area network

00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:17.470
to both bring data
into the platform

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:19.800
as well as to serve
content out, so there's

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:22.380
edge caching built in.

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:24.060
And then another super
important feature

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:27.359
here is consistency,
strong consistency.

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:30.930
So when you write an object
into Google Cloud Storage,

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:35.340
it's immediately available for
all of the compute instances

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:40.961
or analytics instances to see
it immediately and operate over

00:17:40.961 --> 00:17:41.460
it.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:43.668
I'll talk a little bit more
about strong and eventual

00:17:43.668 --> 00:17:46.640
consistency, but
strong consistency

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:48.390
is super important for
analytics workloads

00:17:48.390 --> 00:17:50.973
when you've got multiple compute
instances reading and writing

00:17:50.973 --> 00:17:52.200
against a single bucket.

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:54.830
They all see the same
view of the data.

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:57.800
And that's a pretty big deal
for analytics workloads.

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:00.070
That's Google Cloud Storage.

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:00.790
Persistent Disk.

00:18:00.790 --> 00:18:04.770
So this is the classic
I'm running a VM,

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:09.360
I'm using containers,
and I need a disk.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:11.100
So the disk that we
provide you, it's

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:12.780
not like a physical
disk that's actually

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:15.010
on the same server
in our data centers.

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:16.570
It's actually over the network.

00:18:16.570 --> 00:18:20.070
We create you, effectively, a
virtual disk that you can then

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:22.830
attach to these instances.

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:27.030
We provide it as a
single zonal product.

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:30.240
And then we've just
also recently announced

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:35.490
a beta of what we call Regional
Persistent Disk, which actually

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:38.640
replicates synchronously
the data between two zones

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:41.640
within a region, which is
super important for workloads

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:43.520
like databases,
where you might want

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:47.270
to think about high availability
and failover of the database

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:49.550
instance.

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:51.900
So again, we make this
super simple to use.

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:55.670
We offer two tiers, standard
and high performance tiers.

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:58.160
With a single disk, you
can scale it dynamically up

00:18:58.160 --> 00:18:59.984
to 64 terabytes.

00:18:59.984 --> 00:19:01.400
And we're announcing
at this event

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:05.060
that you can go up to
60,000 IOPS on that.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:07.790
And again, one thing that's
super interesting, super unique

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:11.720
here is that you don't need
to stripe your disks to get

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:13.490
the maximum performance
out of the VM,

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:16.030
so classic on-premises
IT infrastructure.

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:17.660
You attach multiple disks.

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:19.845
You set up striping,
raid, and other things

00:19:19.845 --> 00:19:21.470
from an IT administration
point of view

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:24.020
to get the behavior
that you want.

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:27.560
You don't need to do any of that
with Persistent Disk on GCP.

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:30.080
We take care of all of that
behind the scenes for you.

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:31.760
You just create a single disk.

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:33.030
You run all your workloads.

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:37.130
We balance all of the
operations against it,

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:40.910
and then let you
seamlessly scale it up

00:19:40.910 --> 00:19:45.380
to the maximum
performance of the VM.

00:19:45.380 --> 00:19:47.510
We also have
management operations,

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:51.630
like being able to take
snapshots against the data.

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:54.140
You can also take these
disks and attach them

00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:59.420
to multiple VMs in read-only
mode for often sharing data.

00:19:59.420 --> 00:20:03.500
So again, it's super
simple, super powerful,

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:05.540
easy-to-use product
is what we're

00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:07.700
aiming for here with our
Persistent Disk for block

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:10.120
storage.

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:10.620
Filestore.

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:13.827
So Cloud Filestore, if
you remember earlier,

00:20:13.827 --> 00:20:15.410
I was talking about
the different ways

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:18.290
you might want to access storage
effectively over the network,

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:19.820
and how you think about that.

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:21.440
Lots of applications
have been written

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:23.600
with file storage in mind.

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:31.490
So this is file shares, or I'm
doing video and image editing

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:32.490
in my organization.

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:35.500
I need to share
files between them.

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:39.260
Or I might be-- once I've
done some manipulation

00:20:39.260 --> 00:20:41.690
of various image files, I
want to do some rendering

00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:43.260
in a render farm.

00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:48.710
And I need lots of compute to
access those files, or just

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:50.870
general sort of web
applications like WordPress.

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:52.910
They all operate on
this model of basically

00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:54.360
a shared file system.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:57.320
So with Cloud Filestore,
this gives customers

00:20:57.320 --> 00:21:01.610
now a fully managed network
attached file storage--

00:21:01.610 --> 00:21:04.340
again, standard and
performance tiers.

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:06.260
We focused very much
on making it easy,

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:09.350
simple to use from both
the price and performance

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:13.460
point of view, integrate
it well with GC and GKE,

00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:18.370
and give really just good,
solid, consistent IOPS

00:21:18.370 --> 00:21:20.610
and performance.

00:21:20.610 --> 00:21:21.590
So this is now in beta.

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:25.100
We announced it
about a month ago.

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:27.300
All right, over to the
world of databases.

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:29.750
So I wondered how
far to go here,

00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:32.660
ACID properties, cap
theorem, all the kind

00:21:32.660 --> 00:21:35.900
of things you want in an intro
talk on where to store storage.

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:38.660
But I thought I better hit
a couple of the concepts

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:41.880
just because it will help
talk about them later.

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:45.110
So traditional relational
database management system,

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:50.090
you create tables of data,
and they have defined columns.

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:52.760
And then you set constraints--
foreign keys, for example--

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:53.780
between them.

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:57.530
Because you want to do things
like, if I've got a savings,

00:21:57.530 --> 00:22:00.680
if I'm a bank, and customers
have multiple accounts with me,

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:05.040
and I want to deduct dollars
from my savings account

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:06.830
and put it in my
checking account,

00:22:06.830 --> 00:22:09.470
I've got to make multiple
operations on multiple rows

00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:10.610
at the same time.

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:12.960
And I want to make sure
that when I do that,

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:14.556
the whole thing all
happens together,

00:22:14.556 --> 00:22:16.680
even though it's happening
across different tables.

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:19.470
So that's called a transaction.

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:21.320
Transactions have
particular properties,

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:23.000
ACID properties, atomic.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:25.250
If I'm moving data
between two accounts,

00:22:25.250 --> 00:22:28.020
all of those operations need to
happen at the same time, right?

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:28.645
So it's atomic.

00:22:28.645 --> 00:22:30.220
They all happen together.

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:33.440
Consistent, I'm guaranteed that
when the operation completes,

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:36.500
I leave the multiple tables
and the overall model

00:22:36.500 --> 00:22:39.200
of the database in
a consistent state.

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:44.240
Isolated means if you have
several operations or writing

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:47.589
transactions at
the same time, then

00:22:47.589 --> 00:22:49.130
they won't interfere
with each other.

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:52.420
And durable says that
once you ACK back

00:22:52.420 --> 00:22:55.389
that that operation is
complete, that it really,

00:22:55.389 --> 00:22:56.180
really is complete.

00:22:56.180 --> 00:22:58.520
Even if the system
dies, it guarantees

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:01.250
to be able to recover
to that point in time.

00:23:01.250 --> 00:23:05.010
So this is the classic
properties of transactions.

00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:06.820
And I talked a little
bit earlier about

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:11.480
as NoSQL solutions have come
along, some of the trade-offs

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:12.230
that they've made.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:14.180
And one of the big
things is, as I

00:23:14.180 --> 00:23:16.990
think about really wide
columns or document storage,

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:20.270
they, frankly, often don't
need all of those capabilities.

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:23.030
And so we're willing to
make some trade-offs.

00:23:23.030 --> 00:23:25.700
And one of the most fundamental
trade-offs they often make

00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:29.780
is between strong and
eventual consistency.

00:23:29.780 --> 00:23:31.280
Just to kind of
confuse things, this

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:33.080
is a different
type of consistency

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:35.480
than the consistency
on the left.

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:37.370
So strong eventual
consistency means,

00:23:37.370 --> 00:23:41.660
as I'm writing data in,
whoever reads it is guaranteed

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:42.832
to see the latest version.

00:23:42.832 --> 00:23:45.290
So strong consistency says
every time something is written,

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:50.060
all of the readers see the
latest version of the object.

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:53.390
With eventual consistency,
it means eventually everybody

00:23:53.390 --> 00:23:57.560
will see the latest version,
but not immediately.

00:23:57.560 --> 00:24:00.710
And that might sound weird,
but there's some use cases

00:24:00.710 --> 00:24:05.960
where, if I'm following how
many people like or follow me

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:10.450
on Twitter or something,
whether it's 50,000 or 50,001,

00:24:10.450 --> 00:24:14.290
I don't really care as
long as it's really fast

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:19.790
and gets me good enough
results quickly, right?

00:24:19.790 --> 00:24:22.190
So I'm willing to make some
trade-offs on accuracy.

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:27.177
But if I'm looking at banking
records, I don't want to--

00:24:27.177 --> 00:24:27.760
no, thank you.

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:29.870
I really want to know
that what I am looking at

00:24:29.870 --> 00:24:33.800
is the latest copy of the
data, thank you very much.

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:36.410
So NoSQL systems have made
some of these trade-offs.

00:24:36.410 --> 00:24:40.571
And I'll talk about the products
a little bit from that context.

00:24:40.571 --> 00:24:43.070
So let me start with just the
relational database management

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:44.000
systems.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:49.490
So Cloud SQL is our
product for fully managed

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:51.050
third-party relational DBs.

00:24:51.050 --> 00:24:54.860
So if you're using MySQL,
if you're using Postgres,

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:59.660
if you are kind of sick of
dealing with underlying storage

00:24:59.660 --> 00:25:02.810
concepts of replication,
clustering, and failover,

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:04.985
then Cloud SQL is
the product for you.

00:25:04.985 --> 00:25:07.760
It gives you all of
the ACID transactions

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:10.520
at the database level that you'd
be familiar with with an RDBMS

00:25:10.520 --> 00:25:11.290
system.

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:17.060
It gets you out of having
to manage the underlying

00:25:17.060 --> 00:25:20.330
back ends to deal with
high availability,

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:22.460
backup, security
updates, gets you out

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:25.700
of the infrastructure management
and focused on the higher level

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:26.690
values of their app.

00:25:26.690 --> 00:25:30.440
You can, of course, take
MySQL and run it in a GC VM,

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:33.320
and attach a disk to it, and
do this yourself, if you want.

00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:38.180
But with Cloud SQL,
we let you worry

00:25:38.180 --> 00:25:41.630
less about managing and
keeping that service running,

00:25:41.630 --> 00:25:44.030
and focus on the higher
level applications,

00:25:44.030 --> 00:25:47.090
so used for all of
the classic examples

00:25:47.090 --> 00:25:49.850
that you would expect a
good, old relational database

00:25:49.850 --> 00:25:51.920
management system
to be used for.

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:57.230
One interesting thing with most
relational database management

00:25:57.230 --> 00:25:58.938
systems is they're
basically what I would

00:25:58.938 --> 00:26:00.350
call scale-up systems, right?

00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:03.380
So if you want
more transactions,

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:07.740
if you want more
storage, you kind of

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:09.740
have to just keep building
a bigger, and bigger,

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:10.985
and bigger system.

00:26:10.985 --> 00:26:14.420
HP, for many years, would build
bigger and bigger superdomes

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:16.310
and other gigantic
systems basically

00:26:16.310 --> 00:26:19.521
to run really, really, really,
really, really big databases.

00:26:19.521 --> 00:26:21.020
And the reason they
did that is it's

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:23.260
really hard to scale
out a database,

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:25.160
because as soon as you
scale out a database,

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:27.860
you end up with data
in multiple places.

00:26:27.860 --> 00:26:31.760
And remember, in a relational
database management system,

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:36.290
you want to keep a consistent
view of the entire database.

00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:37.100
You've got rows.

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:38.340
You've got tables.

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:40.630
But the unit of consistency
from an ACID point of view

00:26:40.630 --> 00:26:42.500
is an entire database.

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:46.800
And as that scales out, it
becomes very, very challenging

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:48.620
to manage that.

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:51.110
In fact, the cap
theorem basically

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:54.360
tells you you get to
pick between consistency,

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:56.420
availability, and
ability to partition.

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:59.240
And you only get to pick
two out of the three, right?

00:26:59.240 --> 00:27:03.590
So one of the things
we've built at Google,

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:06.530
we've built it internally
to deal with this problem,

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:09.080
is a product called
Cloud Spanner.

00:27:09.080 --> 00:27:14.630
And as you scale your systems
up from an RDBMS perspective,

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:17.690
imagine you want to deal with
a global inventory system.

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:20.330
And you've got systems all
around the world managing

00:27:20.330 --> 00:27:23.720
inventory, but you want a
single, common global inventory

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:25.961
system.

00:27:25.961 --> 00:27:27.710
What customers would
typically start to do

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:30.230
is they'd start to
manage, and build,

00:27:30.230 --> 00:27:33.560
and shard, and come up with
these complex application

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:38.060
constructs to effectively
shard out their RDBMS solution.

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:40.910
So it would be one
application, but they'd

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:43.340
have five different
database servers

00:27:43.340 --> 00:27:46.100
that we're dealing with
different parts of it.

00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:49.160
And the edge condition--
it just becomes

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:50.750
horrible and gnarly and messy.

00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:55.070
If you've ever tried it,
good job, because it's

00:27:55.070 --> 00:27:56.750
really, really hard to do.

00:27:56.750 --> 00:27:58.314
And you can wire
it together to work

00:27:58.314 --> 00:27:59.480
for particular applications.

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:02.379
But it's just not where you
want to be spending your time.

00:28:02.379 --> 00:28:03.920
So what we've done
with Cloud Spanner

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:06.980
is actually build a fully
managed relational database

00:28:06.980 --> 00:28:10.070
system that can do
transactions at the database

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:13.297
level at a global scale.

00:28:13.297 --> 00:28:15.380
And the thing that we've
done that's really unique

00:28:15.380 --> 00:28:17.360
is-- you have to make
some trade-off, right?

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:20.660
And what we've
done is, by using--

00:28:20.660 --> 00:28:24.950
this is a fascinating story--
atomic clocks and something

00:28:24.950 --> 00:28:27.080
called TrueTime,
we've made it so

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:29.450
that the trade-off
on availability

00:28:29.450 --> 00:28:31.987
is so tiny that it doesn't
matter from an application

00:28:31.987 --> 00:28:34.070
point of view, to all
intents and purposes, right?

00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:36.230
So you've got a fully
consistent database.

00:28:36.230 --> 00:28:38.700
We partitioned it
across the world.

00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:41.810
And we've used atomic clocks
to get synchronization so tight

00:28:41.810 --> 00:28:44.380
that we can still get a
five nines availability

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:45.890
as a layer over this data.

00:28:45.890 --> 00:28:49.790
So this is an incredibly
powerful relational database

00:28:49.790 --> 00:28:52.220
management system
that we've been

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:56.404
using to run some of the largest
systems inside of Google.

00:28:56.404 --> 00:28:58.070
I'm really excited
to see what customers

00:28:58.070 --> 00:28:59.153
are going to do with this.

00:28:59.153 --> 00:29:01.430
This was just beta-ed
a year ago, GA now,

00:29:01.430 --> 00:29:05.427
so seeing some super
interesting pick up on this one.

00:29:05.427 --> 00:29:06.260
All right, Bigtable.

00:29:06.260 --> 00:29:09.470
OK, so that was relational
database management systems.

00:29:09.470 --> 00:29:12.800
Now let me switch over
to the NoSQL world.

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:14.190
So let me start with Bigtable.

00:29:14.190 --> 00:29:21.870
So here I want to store lots of
records with very wide columns.

00:29:21.870 --> 00:29:23.990
So I might have
tens, 100s, 1,000s,

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:26.454
tens of thousands of different
columns that I might want.

00:29:26.454 --> 00:29:28.370
Not all the data is going
to have all columns.

00:29:28.370 --> 00:29:30.400
It's going to be very
sparsely populated.

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:31.940
It's not very
efficient to pack that

00:29:31.940 --> 00:29:35.000
into a relational database.

00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:38.540
And the thing that I care about
is that the unit of atomicity--

00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:42.870
remember, in a database,
you've got rows and tables

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:44.290
in the entire database.

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:49.250
In a wide column
database like Bigtable,

00:29:49.250 --> 00:29:51.350
really, you're only
getting atomic operations

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:52.430
at the row level, right?

00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:57.740
So you don't get foreign key
relationships or anything fancy

00:29:57.740 --> 00:29:58.250
like that.

00:29:58.250 --> 00:30:02.450
What you get is the ability to
write and read row-level data

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:03.870
incredibly quickly.

00:30:03.870 --> 00:30:06.910
So this has a single-digit
millisecond reads and writes.

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:11.810
And the trade-off
there is, if you

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:16.500
want to read all of that data,
it's eventually consistent.

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:19.580
So it's good for sort of big
aggregate-type operations.

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:22.714
If you've been using something
like Cassandra or HBase,

00:30:22.714 --> 00:30:24.380
which have really
large amounts of data,

00:30:24.380 --> 00:30:26.690
and you're having trouble
scaling those clusters,

00:30:26.690 --> 00:30:28.580
then we have a
HBase-compatible API

00:30:28.580 --> 00:30:31.790
that lets you very easily
move those applications across

00:30:31.790 --> 00:30:34.130
to run over Bigtable.

00:30:34.130 --> 00:30:36.140
Really great for sort
of real-time analytics

00:30:36.140 --> 00:30:39.700
workloads, building out,
monitoring time series,

00:30:39.700 --> 00:30:40.760
IoT data sets.

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:46.190
Next, if I now move
from, hey, I care

00:30:46.190 --> 00:30:49.130
about an individual
row to I have

00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:55.444
now a document-like structure
for user profiles, messaging,

00:30:55.444 --> 00:30:57.110
product catalogs,
typically, I'm dealing

00:30:57.110 --> 00:31:03.020
with sort of a JSON structure
where I've got a tree of data.

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:05.120
And what I want
to deal with here

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:09.020
is I want a certain amount
of transactional support.

00:31:09.020 --> 00:31:11.030
It doesn't need to be
over my entire system,

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:13.850
but I'm going to get it
over a subset of my data.

00:31:13.850 --> 00:31:15.530
And eventual consistency is OK.

00:31:15.530 --> 00:31:21.150
And this is where the
current Datastore product is.

00:31:21.150 --> 00:31:24.710
And if you look at products
to compare with out there,

00:31:24.710 --> 00:31:28.690
if I'm running
MongoDB or Couchbase,

00:31:28.690 --> 00:31:30.780
then I'm running those clusters.

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:32.650
This is a fully managed
version of that.

00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:33.570
So now you wouldn't
need to worry

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:35.486
about managing the
clusters if you switch over

00:31:35.486 --> 00:31:37.080
to using Datastore.

00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:39.240
There's a couple of other
things on this page which

00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:40.770
I'll highlight now.

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:42.847
So at this event,
we're talking about how

00:31:42.847 --> 00:31:45.180
we're going to move from what
we call data storage, sort

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:47.010
of second generation
product here.

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:50.100
We're going to be moving to what
we call the third generation

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:52.897
product in Firestore, so
there's a little asterisk there.

00:31:52.897 --> 00:31:54.480
So you'll see we
have something called

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:55.614
Cloud Firestore announced.

00:31:55.614 --> 00:31:57.030
What we're going
to do is actually

00:31:57.030 --> 00:32:00.120
we'll carry forward Datastore
completely backwards compatible

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:00.910
within that.

00:32:00.910 --> 00:32:02.760
But we're actually
going to bring even

00:32:02.760 --> 00:32:04.710
more of underlying power to it.

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:07.500
So you can still use
that document data model,

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:09.630
but we're going to remove
some of the restrictions

00:32:09.630 --> 00:32:13.120
that you have with it today.

00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:15.120
So you'll be able to do
transactional support

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:18.660
across the entire store.

00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:21.090
And we'll move from an
eventual to strong consistency

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:22.542
model for this as well.

00:32:22.542 --> 00:32:24.750
Remember when I was saying
earlier the world of NoSQL

00:32:24.750 --> 00:32:27.090
database is
continuing to evolve?

00:32:27.090 --> 00:32:30.705
Evolution in
progress right here.

00:32:30.705 --> 00:32:34.350
The key thing here
is your data model

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:37.500
is much more of a
document-structured data model.

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:38.510
All right, memorystore.

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:42.021
So great for storing
all of your data,

00:32:42.021 --> 00:32:43.770
run through a whole
bunch of stores there.

00:32:43.770 --> 00:32:46.690
Now I just want to make my
application go really, really,

00:32:46.690 --> 00:32:47.410
really fast.

00:32:47.410 --> 00:32:50.070
So you might use something
like Redis for this already.

00:32:50.070 --> 00:32:52.050
We offer, basically, a
fully managed version

00:32:52.050 --> 00:32:56.130
of that Memorystore, so you
can now cache data in memory

00:32:56.130 --> 00:33:00.600
to give you millisecond
lookups for that data

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:01.850
from your application.

00:33:01.850 --> 00:33:03.160
Of course, it's not persistent.

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:04.530
The persistence is in
the stores I've already

00:33:04.530 --> 00:33:06.720
talked about, but super
fast to accelerate

00:33:06.720 --> 00:33:09.260
a bunch of your workloads.

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:10.706
BigQuery.

00:33:10.706 --> 00:33:15.380
So I've talked a lot about
regular storage, databases,

00:33:15.380 --> 00:33:16.590
and now data warehouse.

00:33:16.590 --> 00:33:20.850
So BigQuery is our massively
scalable data warehouse

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:22.350
workload.

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:27.000
You can store gigabytes to
exabytes of the data in this,

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:30.479
running really large-scale
analytics over it.

00:33:30.479 --> 00:33:32.270
I've been with Google
nearly two years now.

00:33:32.270 --> 00:33:34.260
This is one of my
favorite internal products

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:35.370
when I got here.

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:37.680
I was looking
around and querying,

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:39.420
who's using what
in the products?

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:40.380
So I write a query.

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:40.920
I run it.

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:43.530
15, 20 seconds later, a
result set comes back.

00:33:43.530 --> 00:33:45.240
I'm like, oh, that's cool.

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:46.990
How much data did I just query?

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:49.840
I do the count, select.

00:33:49.840 --> 00:33:51.420
There's several billion records.

00:33:51.420 --> 00:33:54.640
I'm like, oh, yeah,
that's pretty cool.

00:33:54.640 --> 00:34:00.600
So this is the really scalable,
powerful data warehouse product

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:01.170
that we have.

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:04.530
It has an optimized
column of storage format

00:34:04.530 --> 00:34:07.350
that we manage on your behalf to
just make it work really fast.

00:34:07.350 --> 00:34:10.620
You can stream data in at
a really fast rate whilst

00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:12.659
querying it at the same time.

00:34:12.659 --> 00:34:14.429
We just announced
in the keynote today

00:34:14.429 --> 00:34:17.429
that you can actually now build
ML models directly in BigQuery

00:34:17.429 --> 00:34:18.870
without leaving BigQuery.

00:34:18.870 --> 00:34:20.489
This is a really big deal.

00:34:20.489 --> 00:34:23.460
It's really hard for
people to do data science.

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:25.889
This just makes it
easier to build models

00:34:25.889 --> 00:34:28.500
so that your organization
can start to leverage them

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:32.530
right from within the tool
that you're already using.

00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:35.404
So this is used across
very many workloads,

00:34:35.404 --> 00:34:37.320
often as a complement
to something like a data

00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:39.736
lake, where I'm storing large
amounts of unstructured data

00:34:39.736 --> 00:34:44.159
for surveying and for building
the classic sort of data

00:34:44.159 --> 00:34:48.659
warehouse solution on GCP.

00:34:48.659 --> 00:34:51.900
And I think, with that, I'm
going to hand over to Robert,

00:34:51.900 --> 00:34:53.726
who will take it away.

00:34:53.726 --> 00:34:56.100
ROBERT SAXBY: So now we've
gone through all the storages.

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:58.560
I guess, like
everyone else, we all

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:00.041
know which stores to pick now.

00:35:00.041 --> 00:35:00.540
That's it.

00:35:00.540 --> 00:35:01.530
We're done.

00:35:01.530 --> 00:35:03.870
So if you know which
storage to pick,

00:35:03.870 --> 00:35:05.820
then that was the
end of the talk.

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:09.870
If you're like me and many
customers I've worked with,

00:35:09.870 --> 00:35:11.490
you think, oh, I get it now.

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:12.996
This is what I'm going to use.

00:35:12.996 --> 00:35:14.370
And then someone
comes up to you,

00:35:14.370 --> 00:35:16.005
but we tried to
use this product.

00:35:16.005 --> 00:35:17.880
Well, hopefully we're
going to try and tackle

00:35:17.880 --> 00:35:20.820
some of that now, and provide a
little bit more context of how

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:22.406
to choose which storage.

00:35:22.406 --> 00:35:24.780
And there are different use
cases we're going to look at.

00:35:24.780 --> 00:35:27.150
I mean, some of these are more
of a lift-and-shift type of use

00:35:27.150 --> 00:35:28.733
case, and some of
them are going to be

00:35:28.733 --> 00:35:32.680
more of the next
generation of application.

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:39.764
Let me just grab a hold
of this, or even this one.

00:35:39.764 --> 00:35:42.050
There we go.

00:35:42.050 --> 00:35:43.820
So the first couple
I'll show you

00:35:43.820 --> 00:35:46.640
are more of a
lift-and-shift type nature.

00:35:46.640 --> 00:35:50.360
So one use case, or one
expansion into the cloud

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:53.180
that we see, even if we're
not talking about applications

00:35:53.180 --> 00:35:55.670
or different
workloads, is basically

00:35:55.670 --> 00:35:58.670
archiving data,
disaster recovery,

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:05.030
and just making that data
available for some other use

00:36:05.030 --> 00:36:07.380
case somewhere else later.

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:09.110
So in this particular
use case, it's

00:36:09.110 --> 00:36:13.490
basically how do we get data
from on premise to the cloud?

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:16.760
So the typical place
where this data lands

00:36:16.760 --> 00:36:18.756
is Google Cloud Storage.

00:36:18.756 --> 00:36:21.325
As Dave pointed out,
it's object storage.

00:36:24.250 --> 00:36:27.971
The durability is
something like 11 nines,

00:36:27.971 --> 00:36:30.470
so you don't have to worry about
backing that data up there.

00:36:30.470 --> 00:36:32.750
This is highly durable storage.

00:36:32.750 --> 00:36:35.190
So things that you
might want to do here

00:36:35.190 --> 00:36:37.430
is, if I'm going to
use it for an archive,

00:36:37.430 --> 00:36:41.030
how often do I actually
go and access that data?

00:36:41.030 --> 00:36:44.300
So as Dave pointed out, we have
different tiers for pricing

00:36:44.300 --> 00:36:45.737
there on a single API.

00:36:45.737 --> 00:36:46.820
So this makes it all good.

00:36:46.820 --> 00:36:49.680
But then the next question is,
how do I get my data there?

00:36:49.680 --> 00:36:51.590
So to get your data to
Google Cloud Storage,

00:36:51.590 --> 00:36:54.230
we provide utilities
like gsutil, which I'm

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:56.000
sure some of you already use.

00:36:56.000 --> 00:36:58.160
We have the transfer
service, which

00:36:58.160 --> 00:37:02.240
is for scheduling larger loads,
and you can check the cache

00:37:02.240 --> 00:37:06.200
codes so that you're actually
reading the right objects,

00:37:06.200 --> 00:37:08.780
bringing the right
things across.

00:37:08.780 --> 00:37:11.240
And we have a whole bunch
of partners out there.

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:13.580
And I just really
put a few of them

00:37:13.580 --> 00:37:15.530
up here just to kind of
illustrate the point.

00:37:15.530 --> 00:37:18.200
They really operate in the
different areas, so ingesting

00:37:18.200 --> 00:37:20.870
data, helping provide
solutions that backup

00:37:20.870 --> 00:37:22.620
and do disaster
recovery, so you're

00:37:22.620 --> 00:37:24.620
getting your data somewhere,
but then getting it

00:37:24.620 --> 00:37:26.040
back again as well.

00:37:26.040 --> 00:37:28.250
And obviously, in
that middle category,

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:29.930
you're also thinking,
what application

00:37:29.930 --> 00:37:31.290
am I taking the data from?

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:34.040
So you might not be on premise,
reading it from object storage.

00:37:34.040 --> 00:37:35.840
You might be reading
it from a NAS.

00:37:35.840 --> 00:37:38.146
You might be reading it
from a database system.

00:37:38.146 --> 00:37:39.520
So how do I get
that storage out?

00:37:39.520 --> 00:37:40.880
You might be reading logs.

00:37:40.880 --> 00:37:43.340
How do I get that storage
out into the cloud?

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:46.340
So I know I've got my
backup off premise now,

00:37:46.340 --> 00:37:48.720
I know I've got a
disaster recovery plan,

00:37:48.720 --> 00:37:50.300
and I can start
archiving as well.

00:37:50.300 --> 00:37:53.914
I can start storing maybe
less data on premise.

00:37:53.914 --> 00:37:55.580
So these are some of
the things that you

00:37:55.580 --> 00:37:57.980
can consider with using
Google Cloud Storage.

00:37:57.980 --> 00:38:00.780
And that typically tends to
be the storage medium that

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:04.031
is used in this case.

00:38:04.031 --> 00:38:05.780
So the next one is a
bit more complicated,

00:38:05.780 --> 00:38:08.850
because it has basically
gone horizontal on all

00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:11.380
the different types of
storage we have here.

00:38:11.380 --> 00:38:14.802
So let's look at-- actually,
we were having the conversation

00:38:14.802 --> 00:38:16.260
when we were putting
this together.

00:38:16.260 --> 00:38:18.990
We were trying to define,
what is lift and shift?

00:38:18.990 --> 00:38:20.970
And it became very
clear to us that there

00:38:20.970 --> 00:38:24.030
were two actual types
of lift and shift.

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:27.880
One is the re-hosting, and
the other is a refactoring.

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:31.020
Now, in rehosting, you
have an application.

00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:33.900
It runs, say, on
Windows or on Linux,

00:38:33.900 --> 00:38:36.600
and you want to run that
application in the cloud.

00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:38.550
Now, that application
requires storage.

00:38:38.550 --> 00:38:40.880
If you wanted to
move, for example,

00:38:40.880 --> 00:38:45.429
that MySequence, and you want
to do your operation yourself,

00:38:45.429 --> 00:38:47.970
and you want to take care of
the maintenance yourself because

00:38:47.970 --> 00:38:50.265
of your specific
requirements or they're

00:38:50.265 --> 00:38:52.890
slightly different from what we
provide in the managed service,

00:38:52.890 --> 00:38:54.150
or you've got
another application

00:38:54.150 --> 00:38:56.340
that we don't have a managed
service for, then you

00:38:56.340 --> 00:38:59.160
would more the application
across into the cloud,

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:00.275
run it on a VM.

00:39:00.275 --> 00:39:01.650
You could run that
in a container

00:39:01.650 --> 00:39:04.080
if it was already containerized,
but classically, this

00:39:04.080 --> 00:39:05.890
tends to be going into a VM.

00:39:05.890 --> 00:39:07.890
And then you've got to
pick what type of storage

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:09.535
do you want underneath.

00:39:09.535 --> 00:39:11.910
The storage in this case, the
two that you could look at,

00:39:11.910 --> 00:39:14.550
are both POSIX block storage.

00:39:14.550 --> 00:39:16.140
You would say
either a local disk,

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:19.530
or if you want to share that
amongst multiple instances,

00:39:19.530 --> 00:39:22.490
like have high availability
when you're running that MySQL,

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:24.900
if you're going to have
that in two different zones,

00:39:24.900 --> 00:39:28.170
and then you would look at the
NAS storage that we provide.

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:31.649
And again, there are partner
solutions for the NAS as well.

00:39:31.649 --> 00:39:34.190
And that might be because you're
already using that partner's

00:39:34.190 --> 00:39:35.762
solution on premise.

00:39:35.762 --> 00:39:37.470
And it would be a very
natural transition

00:39:37.470 --> 00:39:40.990
to use that in the cloud as
well, using the same APIs,

00:39:40.990 --> 00:39:43.350
gathering the same
kind of metrics.

00:39:43.350 --> 00:39:45.650
So lift and refactor,
essentially,

00:39:45.650 --> 00:39:48.150
this is a kind of lift-and-shift
storage where we're saying,

00:39:48.150 --> 00:39:49.510
I'm going to move
across, but I'm

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:51.593
going to be using something
a little bit different

00:39:51.593 --> 00:39:56.250
underneath, because I need
some different capability.

00:39:56.250 --> 00:39:57.580
So let me give an example.

00:39:57.580 --> 00:40:00.590
We start, first of
all, with HBase.

00:40:00.590 --> 00:40:03.450
HBase typically runs
in Hadoop Cluster.

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:04.942
When you want to
scale your HBase,

00:40:04.942 --> 00:40:06.400
then you scale your
Hadoop Cluster.

00:40:06.400 --> 00:40:09.000
You scale the storage,
you scale the compute.

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:12.060
HBase will give you
something like--

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:14.130
given a good key
strategy, so you're not

00:40:14.130 --> 00:40:18.590
hotspottting your keys,
you'll get maybe, 10 to--

00:40:18.590 --> 00:40:22.560
well, somewhat above
10s, probably in 60 to 70

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:25.320
milliseconds of latency
to the first byte.

00:40:25.320 --> 00:40:28.140
With Bigtable,
which will be our--

00:40:28.140 --> 00:40:30.760
and just for the record,
HBase and Cassandra

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:33.770
are based on that
original Bigtable paper.

00:40:33.770 --> 00:40:35.900
So Bigtable will give you,
if you do it properly,

00:40:35.900 --> 00:40:38.610
six milliseconds of latency.

00:40:38.610 --> 00:40:42.120
It's atomic at the row, so
you have strong consistency

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:43.620
at the row level.

00:40:43.620 --> 00:40:46.407
You can now replicate your
Bigtable into multiple zones,

00:40:46.407 --> 00:40:48.990
and then you would have eventual
consistency between those two

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:50.220
instances.

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:52.320
But this thing is
linearly scalable,

00:40:52.320 --> 00:40:55.875
which is quite a hard thing
to do on your HBase cluster.

00:40:55.875 --> 00:41:00.540
And linearly scalable means
that if I have 10,000 IOPS,

00:41:00.540 --> 00:41:01.470
or I want to go--

00:41:01.470 --> 00:41:04.590
we have customers literally
in the hundreds of thousands

00:41:04.590 --> 00:41:05.820
queries per second--

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:06.460
I said IOPS.

00:41:06.460 --> 00:41:08.220
I meant queries
per second, sorry--

00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:11.140
hundreds of thousands of
queries per second at six

00:41:11.140 --> 00:41:14.190
milliseconds latency
across their database.

00:41:14.190 --> 00:41:18.090
This thing is like,
yeah, rocket fuel.

00:41:18.090 --> 00:41:19.050
That's great.

00:41:19.050 --> 00:41:21.330
How do I use it now with
my existing product?

00:41:21.330 --> 00:41:24.450
Well, we actually provide
a HBase-compliant API.

00:41:24.450 --> 00:41:25.990
So the storage is
quite different.

00:41:25.990 --> 00:41:27.698
It gives you some
different capabilities.

00:41:27.698 --> 00:41:29.580
It's much faster.

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:31.890
You can scale it linearly.

00:41:31.890 --> 00:41:33.540
But you have the same API.

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:35.430
So that rehosting
of your application

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:37.800
becomes quite simple.

00:41:37.800 --> 00:41:39.960
Another example
of this is Hadoop.

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:43.960
So if you are moving from
Hadoop, then you're using HDFS.

00:41:43.960 --> 00:41:45.990
Again, you want
to decouple this.

00:41:45.990 --> 00:41:47.923
You want the benefit
of actually being

00:41:47.923 --> 00:41:51.014
able to scale your storage
independently of your compute.

00:41:51.014 --> 00:41:52.680
We would recommend
using object storage.

00:41:52.680 --> 00:41:54.780
Now, there are a
few limitations.

00:41:54.780 --> 00:41:57.300
Object storage is not mutable.

00:41:57.300 --> 00:41:58.540
So you can't do appends.

00:41:58.540 --> 00:42:01.250
You can't do modifications.

00:42:01.250 --> 00:42:04.790
Happens that most Hadoop
workloads now just write out

00:42:04.790 --> 00:42:05.390
new files.

00:42:05.390 --> 00:42:07.190
Think about things like Parquet.

00:42:07.190 --> 00:42:09.420
That works really well
with object storage.

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:12.140
So if you want to move your
Spark job, for example,

00:42:12.140 --> 00:42:16.190
to the cloud, you
run it on Dataproc.

00:42:16.190 --> 00:42:19.010
The only thing you need
to do is to basically run

00:42:19.010 --> 00:42:23.570
that job on Dataproc and
change the HDFS prefix with gs.

00:42:23.570 --> 00:42:26.060
And now suddenly, you're able
to use the object storage.

00:42:26.060 --> 00:42:27.830
You're able to get
much better throughput.

00:42:27.830 --> 00:42:31.760
You can have different workloads
looking at the same storage.

00:42:31.760 --> 00:42:34.650
So you gain some
extra benefits there.

00:42:34.650 --> 00:42:36.290
But there is a slight
rehosting again,

00:42:36.290 --> 00:42:42.000
because you're changing
from HDFS to object storage.

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:47.742
Things like MySQL-- so
you could kind of say this

00:42:47.742 --> 00:42:49.700
is a little bit more
rehosting, but we're going

00:42:49.700 --> 00:42:50.780
to take the management away.

00:42:50.780 --> 00:42:52.613
So that's where the
difference comes in now.

00:42:52.613 --> 00:42:54.410
You would find, for
example, your interface

00:42:54.410 --> 00:42:56.600
with MySQL, Postgres,
or Cloud SQL

00:42:56.600 --> 00:42:58.580
is the same if you were
running it yourself.

00:42:58.580 --> 00:43:01.520
But we've taken care of a
lot of the operational part,

00:43:01.520 --> 00:43:03.720
the backups, and all
this kind of stuff,

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:06.890
so the rehosting part
there is doing less work.

00:43:06.890 --> 00:43:08.960
Sounds terrible.

00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:10.280
And the next one is Redis.

00:43:10.280 --> 00:43:12.650
Again, it's a very
similar kind of thing.

00:43:12.650 --> 00:43:14.810
The rehosting part is that
we are taking away some

00:43:14.810 --> 00:43:18.120
of the operational overhead.

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:21.570
So coming back to the slide,
because I think this is--

00:43:21.570 --> 00:43:24.410
as Dave pointed out, and as
was clear from my examples

00:43:24.410 --> 00:43:26.420
there as well,
this is what makes

00:43:26.420 --> 00:43:28.820
Google really special in
terms of the infrastructure.

00:43:28.820 --> 00:43:31.850
That network in between,
within our data center,

00:43:31.850 --> 00:43:33.920
is 1.3 petabits.

00:43:33.920 --> 00:43:37.730
That means 130,000 machines
talking to each other

00:43:37.730 --> 00:43:39.910
at 10 gigabits per second.

00:43:39.910 --> 00:43:42.500
It's really, really fast.

00:43:42.500 --> 00:43:46.580
That means we can consider
storage which is not local

00:43:46.580 --> 00:43:48.145
effectively local.

00:43:48.145 --> 00:43:50.270
So whether you are attaching
a disk to the machine,

00:43:50.270 --> 00:43:53.120
or you're using object storage,
or you are reading data

00:43:53.120 --> 00:43:57.004
from BigQuery, you're basically
doing that processing power

00:43:57.004 --> 00:43:58.920
separate from where you
are storing your data,

00:43:58.920 --> 00:44:01.470
so multiple workloads
looking at the same data.

00:44:01.470 --> 00:44:05.360
You can scale out the
storage independently

00:44:05.360 --> 00:44:06.680
from the processing.

00:44:06.680 --> 00:44:07.600
That's a given.

00:44:07.600 --> 00:44:09.565
Where this becomes
really concrete

00:44:09.565 --> 00:44:12.950
is, for example, let's say
I need two capabilities.

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:16.260
So I have some data, and
I want to make that data--

00:44:16.260 --> 00:44:19.220
let's say I'm creating
recommendations for people.

00:44:19.220 --> 00:44:21.000
Now, I have two use cases.

00:44:21.000 --> 00:44:26.630
One, I want to be able to
serve those recommendations as

00:44:26.630 --> 00:44:27.827
fast as possible.

00:44:27.827 --> 00:44:29.660
Now, I could choose to
have those in memory,

00:44:29.660 --> 00:44:30.650
in Memorystore.

00:44:30.650 --> 00:44:32.900
I could choose to have
them in a key value store

00:44:32.900 --> 00:44:36.330
like Bigtable, where I've got
random rewrite access and also

00:44:36.330 --> 00:44:38.280
a very low latency.

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:40.370
But let's say I have
another use case as well,

00:44:40.370 --> 00:44:41.690
where I want to do analytics.

00:44:41.690 --> 00:44:45.170
So I want to look at all the
recommendations I've actually

00:44:45.170 --> 00:44:48.550
generated, and I want to see
how they're changing over time,

00:44:48.550 --> 00:44:50.059
all this kind of stuff.

00:44:50.059 --> 00:44:52.100
Well, in that case, it
might be good to have them

00:44:52.100 --> 00:44:54.140
in columnar storage,
because then I

00:44:54.140 --> 00:44:58.460
can do big, massive queries,
and aggregate things,

00:44:58.460 --> 00:45:00.440
and just select the
column I'm interested in,

00:45:00.440 --> 00:45:03.240
instead of selecting
the whole row of data.

00:45:03.240 --> 00:45:06.110
So I would store it in BigQuery.

00:45:06.110 --> 00:45:08.571
If you look at cloud, you look
at the cost of processing,

00:45:08.571 --> 00:45:10.070
and you look at the
cost of storage,

00:45:10.070 --> 00:45:13.050
storage is generally
cheaper than processing.

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:15.750
So it makes sense to store
this data multiple times.

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:17.972
Now, if your use
case is ad hoc, you

00:45:17.972 --> 00:45:19.430
might say, well,
I don't want that.

00:45:19.430 --> 00:45:21.020
Well, then something
like BigQuery

00:45:21.020 --> 00:45:23.330
provides federation to Bigtable.

00:45:23.330 --> 00:45:27.110
If your use case is I need to
run lots and lots of queries

00:45:27.110 --> 00:45:28.725
and I want them
to be really fast,

00:45:28.725 --> 00:45:30.350
then you would store
it in both places.

00:45:30.350 --> 00:45:32.840
So then you might
start to think about,

00:45:32.840 --> 00:45:34.340
how is that pipeline
that's actually

00:45:34.340 --> 00:45:35.881
provisioning that
storage-- how can I

00:45:35.881 --> 00:45:39.580
provision two sets of storage?

00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:42.000
So in this first one, in this
big data and analytics use

00:45:42.000 --> 00:45:47.840
case, we're going to look at
two types of data coming in,

00:45:47.840 --> 00:45:50.560
so both events.

00:45:50.560 --> 00:45:54.030
So this is kind of
streaming data batch.

00:45:54.030 --> 00:45:56.910
Your processing could be
with Beam in Dataflow.

00:45:56.910 --> 00:46:00.570
It could be through Spark and
you're running on Dataproc.

00:46:00.570 --> 00:46:01.830
I'm giving you options here.

00:46:01.830 --> 00:46:03.450
There are more.

00:46:03.450 --> 00:46:06.030
The point is you can
then go and choose

00:46:06.030 --> 00:46:07.814
where you store
this data as well.

00:46:07.814 --> 00:46:10.230
So you could be having your
batch of streaming data coming

00:46:10.230 --> 00:46:12.660
in, you're processing it, and
the next thing you're saying

00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:14.560
is, well, I need to
serve it up quickly.

00:46:14.560 --> 00:46:16.020
So I will move it
across and make

00:46:16.020 --> 00:46:18.520
sure it's available in Bigtable,
that top one in the middle

00:46:18.520 --> 00:46:19.500
there.

00:46:19.500 --> 00:46:21.390
I need to run my
analytics, so you

00:46:21.390 --> 00:46:23.876
make it available in BigQuery.

00:46:23.876 --> 00:46:25.500
Actually, there's a
whole bunch of data

00:46:25.500 --> 00:46:27.732
here I just want to
archive because I

00:46:27.732 --> 00:46:29.190
want to see how
it's created later,

00:46:29.190 --> 00:46:31.120
or it's not in a
structured format yet.

00:46:31.120 --> 00:46:34.390
I would need to
change data types

00:46:34.390 --> 00:46:36.930
or clean the data a bit more
to get it into BigQuery.

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:39.990
Well, then put it in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:46:39.990 --> 00:46:42.090
From there, if you were
going to query that data,

00:46:42.090 --> 00:46:47.940
well, BigQuery is native, so
you would have native storage.

00:46:47.940 --> 00:46:50.460
Your querying is going to
be optimal, the federation

00:46:50.460 --> 00:46:51.790
to the other types of storage.

00:46:51.790 --> 00:46:53.790
And then, from that, you
can go and visualize it

00:46:53.790 --> 00:46:55.789
with a whole host of
different applications,

00:46:55.789 --> 00:46:57.330
or run your models,
or whatever it is

00:46:57.330 --> 00:47:00.490
that you're trying to do there.

00:47:00.490 --> 00:47:02.130
Another example
I want to give is

00:47:02.130 --> 00:47:03.895
a modern, scalable e-commerce.

00:47:03.895 --> 00:47:05.520
You can take away
the word "e-commerce"

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:08.790
here and just basically put
any kind of application that

00:47:08.790 --> 00:47:10.962
you're trying to scale
for internet scale.

00:47:10.962 --> 00:47:12.670
This is how we would
kind of approach it.

00:47:12.670 --> 00:47:15.045
This is really kind of the
benefits of some of the things

00:47:15.045 --> 00:47:16.540
that Google can provide you.

00:47:16.540 --> 00:47:20.190
So for example, our cloud load
balancing-- our load balancing

00:47:20.190 --> 00:47:21.020
is global.

00:47:21.020 --> 00:47:22.860
I mean, we get great
latency on that.

00:47:22.860 --> 00:47:28.920
It will basically allow
you to go and move--

00:47:28.920 --> 00:47:32.340
to route the traffic
to the right.

00:47:32.340 --> 00:47:33.960
You saw earlier in
the presentation

00:47:33.960 --> 00:47:36.890
today how many points
of presence we have.

00:47:36.890 --> 00:47:41.130
Our CDN-- so if you've
got data in your bucket

00:47:41.130 --> 00:47:43.290
in Google Cloud
Storage and you want

00:47:43.290 --> 00:47:44.940
to serve that up
on your website,

00:47:44.940 --> 00:47:46.590
you can put our CDN
in front of that,

00:47:46.590 --> 00:47:48.690
so it's caching it
on our edge, so we

00:47:48.690 --> 00:47:52.680
can serve that up to your
customers as quick as possible.

00:47:52.680 --> 00:47:54.722
And then let's take, for
example, the other type.

00:47:54.722 --> 00:47:56.721
So the other things that
we might be doing there

00:47:56.721 --> 00:47:58.230
is having micro
services, so running

00:47:58.230 --> 00:48:00.180
on Kubernetes, for example.

00:48:00.180 --> 00:48:02.455
And we might want
to have a backbone,

00:48:02.455 --> 00:48:04.830
like a database, a strong,
consistent database that would

00:48:04.830 --> 00:48:05.955
scale with the application.

00:48:05.955 --> 00:48:09.490
It could also be globally
available as well.

00:48:09.490 --> 00:48:12.150
So another point about
Spanner, not only

00:48:12.150 --> 00:48:13.810
does it scale horizontally.

00:48:13.810 --> 00:48:15.707
You can also run this globally.

00:48:15.707 --> 00:48:17.790
That's what we do for
things like AdWords bidding,

00:48:17.790 --> 00:48:19.650
for example.

00:48:19.650 --> 00:48:21.780
Another example--
Spanner underpins

00:48:21.780 --> 00:48:23.650
so much of what we do.

00:48:23.650 --> 00:48:25.350
If you look at
Google Cloud Storage

00:48:25.350 --> 00:48:28.200
and you use that API
to list the items

00:48:28.200 --> 00:48:31.440
in the bucket, that metadata,
again, is stored in Spanner

00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:32.640
as a global product.

00:48:32.640 --> 00:48:35.280
So if you run a
command and say, what

00:48:35.280 --> 00:48:36.780
data do I have in
this bucket, you

00:48:36.780 --> 00:48:39.420
won't expect to see any lag
in getting that result back,

00:48:39.420 --> 00:48:41.115
and you expect a
consistent result.

00:48:41.115 --> 00:48:42.990
So you could use something
like Cloud Spanner

00:48:42.990 --> 00:48:46.520
on your own application and
support multiple micro services

00:48:46.520 --> 00:48:50.070
running in multiple zones, the
load balancer directing you

00:48:50.070 --> 00:48:52.500
to the right microsurfaces
closest to you

00:48:52.500 --> 00:48:55.950
to reduce latency, and having
a strong, consistent data

00:48:55.950 --> 00:48:58.170
model behind that.

00:48:58.170 --> 00:48:59.970
And you may have
things that you want

00:48:59.970 --> 00:49:02.140
to put to get even
faster to your customer.

00:49:02.140 --> 00:49:05.820
So Spanner will give you
in the tens or the low tens

00:49:05.820 --> 00:49:06.945
of milliseconds of latency.

00:49:06.945 --> 00:49:08.403
Maybe you have
things that you want

00:49:08.403 --> 00:49:10.940
to serve up in your application
even faster than that,

00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:12.840
so submillisecond.

00:49:12.840 --> 00:49:14.539
User sessions might
be an example.

00:49:14.539 --> 00:49:16.330
You might have stuff
from a product catalog

00:49:16.330 --> 00:49:18.850
that you really want to get
out as quick as possible.

00:49:18.850 --> 00:49:20.910
And for that, you
could use Memorystore.

00:49:20.910 --> 00:49:22.915
So hopefully this very
high-level architecture

00:49:22.915 --> 00:49:24.790
gives you an idea of
just how you would piece

00:49:24.790 --> 00:49:27.567
some of these things together.

00:49:27.567 --> 00:49:29.400
So the last thing before
we take questions--

00:49:29.400 --> 00:49:31.775
and I realize we're running
out of time, so I will wait--

00:49:31.775 --> 00:49:34.274
and I'm sure Dave will as well--
to answer any questions you

00:49:34.274 --> 00:49:35.072
have--

00:49:35.072 --> 00:49:38.495
is, OK, which storage?

00:49:38.495 --> 00:49:41.730
We've gone through
a few examples.

00:49:41.730 --> 00:49:43.410
We have examples of
this on the website.

00:49:43.410 --> 00:49:46.710
And I kind of went all OCD
on the airplane over here,

00:49:46.710 --> 00:49:47.700
as you can see.

00:49:50.925 --> 00:49:53.580
So something like this should
only take an hour to create.

00:49:53.580 --> 00:49:55.454
But when you want to
fit it all on the screen

00:49:55.454 --> 00:49:57.400
and you move it from
left to right 100 times,

00:49:57.400 --> 00:49:59.160
then you can kill
at least two hours

00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:02.880
on a transatlantic flight.

00:50:02.880 --> 00:50:05.250
But hopefully-- I'll just
leave this up on the screen

00:50:05.250 --> 00:50:07.650
as we wrap up.

00:50:07.650 --> 00:50:09.310
We've spoken about
all of this today.

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:13.440
And this is not the
definitive answer.

00:50:13.440 --> 00:50:16.020
This is a frame, a
guide, a decision tree

00:50:16.020 --> 00:50:18.510
to help you kind of
work out which storage

00:50:18.510 --> 00:50:19.740
to start looking at.

00:50:19.740 --> 00:50:21.970
You'll see, for example,
one storage here, Bigtable,

00:50:21.970 --> 00:50:23.942
you'll see in multiple places.

00:50:23.942 --> 00:50:26.400
One of the questions I get
asked about Bigtable and Spanner

00:50:26.400 --> 00:50:27.525
is, which one should I use?

00:50:27.525 --> 00:50:29.640
I should use Spanner now,
because Spanner is just

00:50:29.640 --> 00:50:32.790
like Bigtable, but then
has strong consistency

00:50:32.790 --> 00:50:34.069
over multiple records.

00:50:34.069 --> 00:50:34.860
Well, yes, it does.

00:50:34.860 --> 00:50:37.620
It has strong consistency
over multiple records,

00:50:37.620 --> 00:50:38.910
over multiple tables.

00:50:38.910 --> 00:50:40.860
With that comes
a lower latency--

00:50:40.860 --> 00:50:42.210
a higher latency.

00:50:42.210 --> 00:50:43.980
Wow.

00:50:43.980 --> 00:50:46.840
So if you need a
very low latency

00:50:46.840 --> 00:50:49.230
and you are only interested
at the row level,

00:50:49.230 --> 00:50:51.160
you could consider Bigtable.

00:50:51.160 --> 00:50:55.190
So these are the kind of things
that you could think about.

00:50:55.190 --> 00:50:56.760
OK, and the rest
of the questions

00:50:56.760 --> 00:51:01.950
we will take outside so the
next speakers can have the room.

00:51:01.950 --> 00:51:05.000
